        "originalSourceTemplateGroup": "I-SUBSTITUTE-POWERS",
        "proposedDistinctReasoningDemand": "Link exponent laws with two simultaneous integer constraints and transfer to a new power expression.",
        "topicId": "math-y9-indices",
        "stem": "Let $x=2^a$ and $y=2^b$, where $a$ and $b$ are integers from $1$ to $8$ inclusive. You are told that $xy=2^5$ and $\\frac{(xy^{-2})^2}{x^{-1}y}=2^{-1}$. What is the exact value of $x^2y$?",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "latex": "128",
            "errorPath": "Swap the values of a and b when evaluating x²y: 2^(2×2+3)=128."
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "latex": "256"
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "latex": "512",
            "errorPath": "Evaluate x³ instead of x²y: 2^(3×3)=512."
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "latex": "32",
            "errorPath": "Calculate xy=2^5 using only the supplied product, instead of the requested x^2y."
          },
          {
            "id": "E",
            "latex": "1024",
            "errorPath": "Compute x^2y^2=2^(2a+2b) after solving, squaring y even though only x should be squared."
          },
          {
            "id": "F",
            "latex": "64",
            "errorPath": "Compute x^2=2^(2a) and forget the remaining multiplication by y=2^2."
          }
        ],
        "correctOptionId": "B",
        "correctAnswerLatex": "256",
        "workedSolutionSteps": [
          "Simplify $\\frac{(xy^{-2})^2}{x^{-1}y}=\\frac{x^2y^{-4}}{x^{-1}y}=x^3y^{-5}$. Substituting $x=2^a,y=2^b$ gives $3a-5b=-1$.",
          "The other condition $xy=2^5$ gives $a+b=5$. Substituting $b=5-a$ into the first equation gives $8a=24$, so $a=3,b=2$.",
          "Therefore $x^2y=(2^3)^2(2^2)=2^8=256$. Both integer bounds are satisfied."
        ],
